Drag icons take the shape of their picture on a screen without compositing.

A drag's icon is a window that follows the pointer.  Where a compositing
manager blends windows, the icon window has an alpha visual and the
transparent parts of the picture show what is under it.  Without one the
window is opaque, and every transparent pixel of the picture showed the
window's background instead: a dragged file icon moved inside a white
rectangle.

Without blending the icon window now takes the shape of its picture.  The
picture is made solid first -- every pixel at least half opaque at full
opacity in its own colour, every other pixel cut away -- and the window's
bounding shape is set from it, so only the picture moves with the pointer
and what is under it shows around it.  This applies to every way a program
sets a drag icon from an image: a cairo surface (which tree views, icon
views and text widgets use as well), a pixbuf, an icon name, a GIcon or a
stock id; those that went through an image widget are drawn into a surface
first.  A drag icon given as a widget is unchanged, and so is everything
on a composited screen.

